  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nLow pressure currently near the Quad Cities will slowly move \nsoutheast today and tonight as it weakens over the Ohio Valley\nSunday. Periods of rain will continue into this evening and then\nslowly end from northwest to southeast tonight into Sunday\nmorning. The rain may be heavy at times through mid/late morning\nwith rain possibly training over the same areas. There will also\nbe the chance for a few thunderstorms, where rainfall rates will\nbe higher. While rainfall amounts of 2+ inches still remains on\ntrack, there is uncertainty for where the highest amounts may\nfall. While the rain will be spread out over several hours, if\nthe heaviest rains fall over more urban areas, the flooding\npotential will be higher. Though the general trend will be for\nprecipitation to slowly end tonight, showers will still be\npossible Sunday morning and perhaps for areas along and east of\nI-55 until they finally push east into northwest IN Sunday\nafternoon.\n\nThere will also be a chance for funnel clouds across the\nsoutheast cwa this afternoon. Not planning any mention in the\nHWO at this time, but the potential will need to be monitored\nlater today.\n\nNortheast winds are expected to gust into the 30-35 mph range\nand will create high waves and dangerous swimming conditions at\nLake Michigan beaches through Monday morning. Given the long \nduration of northeast winds, its possible the beach hazard \nstatement may eventually need to be extended into Monday \nafternoon.\n\nHigh pressure will build back across the region early next week\nbringing dry weather and high temps back in the lower 80s. A\ncold front will move across the Midwest Wednesday into Wednesday\nnight bringing a chance of showers and thunderstorms to the\narea. cms",
